'''Kolb's experiential learning cycle''' is a model of learning through the transformation of experience, associated with David A. Kolb's 1984 book ''Experiential Learning: Experience as the Source of Learning and Development''. It distinguishes four mutually related modes: concrete experience, reflective observation, abstract conceptualization and active experimentation.<ref name="kolb1984">Kolb, David A. (1984). ''Experiential Learning: Experience as the Source of Learning and Development''. Prentice Hall. ISBN 9780132952613.</ref>

{{:Main:Kolb's experiential learning cycle/Simple}}

== Conceptual scope ==
The cycle is a framework for organizing educational experience and reflection, '''not''' a demonstrated universal sequence or a diagnostic classification of immutable learners. Its four modes are often illustrated as a loop, but real learning can be iterative, social, and non-sequential.<ref name="amee2012">Yardley, Sarah; Teunissen, Pim W.; Dornan, Tim (2012). Experiential learning: AMEE Guide No. 63. ''Medical Teacher''. doi:10.3109/0142159X.2012.650741. https://doi.org/10.3109/0142159X.2012.650741</ref>

== Relationship to learning styles ==
Kolb also proposed preferred combinations of learning modes and developed a Learning Style Inventory. Those assessment claims must be evaluated separately from the cycle as a teaching heuristic. Reviews do not establish that assigning instruction to a learner's supposed style reliably improves achievement.<ref name="coffield2004">Coffield, Frank; Moseley, David; Hall, Elaine; Ecclestone, Kathryn (2004). ''Learning styles and pedagogy in post-16 learning: A systematic and critical review''. Learning and Skills Research Centre. https://blogs.northampton.ac.uk/learntech/wp-content/uploads/sites/7/2016/06/Coffield-2004-Learning-styles-and-pedagogy-in-post-16-learning.pdf</ref>

